  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Sunday)\nIssued at 1122 AM CST MON NOV 2 2026\n\nThe KBMX radar picked up on a few patches of light rain/drizzle \nmaking their way across Central Alabama earlier this morning. \nThis checks out based on the 12Z KBMX soundings which reveals a \nnearly fully saturated lower layer of the atmosphere. Otherwise \nfor today, weak upper level ridging will briefly work into the \nregion, keeping us generally rain-free today.\n\nChances for showers and storms will return to the forecast\nTuesday evening as moisture pools ahead of our next system. A\nstout upper level trough will lift across the Plains towards the\nGreat Lakes region. Meanwhile, an associated surface low will\nkick a cold front our way by mid week. Look for breezy conditions\nahead of this front with gusts from 20-30 MPH likely. A line of\nshowers and storms ahead of this front will head our way Tuesday\nevening. Latest guidance suggests this activity will be losing\nsteam by the time it arrives across Central Alabama. While\ninstability will be limited, roughly 30 knots of shear would\nsupport a couple of isolated thunderstorms overnight Tuesday into\nWednesday afternoon. With that said, guidance is hinting at a\ncorridor of higher instability creeping into our southwestern\ncounties so we will need to keep an eye out down there for some\nstronger storms.\n\nWhile the hydro threat appears to be waning, there remains a low\nchance for this system to turn into a flooding event for\nnorthwest portions of Central Alabama. As the line of showers and\nstorms pulls away from its support, it will slow down as it moves\nacross the Southeast. A healthy plume of PWATs and a developing\n~40 knot H85 LLJ will help support periods of heavy rainfall.\nLatest probabilities suggest a 50% chance of seeing at least 2\"\nand a 20% of seeing at least 3\" of rain across our northwestern\ncounties overnight Tuesday through Wednesday. Activity will\ngradually come to an end Wednesday evening with some wrap around\nshowers possible on Thursday.\n\nOur pattern remains active as we head through the late stages of\nthe week. A series of upper troughs and cold fronts will work\nthrough the region over the weekend. Moisture will be limited so\nnot expected too much in the way of precip. For now, we will\ncarry low (10-15%) PoPs. These fronts will bring a refreshing\nshot of cooler air to the region with highs in the mid 60s to low\n70s and lows in the mid 30s to mid 40s.\n\n95/Castillo",
